[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce a DNS or web outage. From host_1_1_1_1, repeated lookups and page loads are consistently fast and error-free: curl to http://web0.local shows name lookup ≈1–2 ms and total ≈3–4 ms; direct IP loads are equally fast. getent hosts for web[0-3].local resolves instantly and repeatedly. dig queries sent directly to the DNS server (10.200.0.2) return authoritative A records with 1h TTL in 0–3 ms. ICMP to the DNS server shows 0% loss. On the DNS server, TCP/UDP 53 is listening and the interface/routing are normal.

The symptom described (sometimes instant, sometimes long “spinning,” then fine later) fits a client-side name resolution policy issue with .local, not a server overload. Many operating systems try mDNS/LLMNR first for “.local” names, waiting on multicast timeouts before falling back to unicast DNS; subsequent attempts seem “fixed” due to resolver cache. Our Linux lab host does not use mDNS (nsswitch: hosts files dns), so it behaves correctly; other endpoints (e.g., Windows/macOS) commonly exhibit the intermittent delay.

Root cause: the internal domain uses .local, which conflicts with mDNS and causes intermittent client-side resolution delays.

Corrective fix: do not restart DNS. Migrate internal zones away from .local (use a delegated subdomain like corp.example or .internal.test). As a mitigation, disable mDNS/LLMNR on clients via policy, or ensure resolvers are ordered to prefer unicast DNS over mDNS. === END ===